  "image_name": "526fb4fc91496ada4c400f2b1785cf21.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how ligand properties and ligand field strength influence metal complexes. At the top left, simple cartoons highlight key ligand features—size, donor/acceptor behavior, and chelation—showing that ligands can differ in how many atoms bind the metal and how strongly they interact. At the top right, a solvation sketch compares more compact, highly charged complexes with less compact ones, linking higher charge density to lower entropy and lower stability in solution, while lower charge density is associated with higher entropy and greater solution stability.\n\nThe large lower section explains ligand-field theory with molecular orbital diagrams. It contrasts strong-field ligands, such as π-acceptors, with weak-field ligands, such as π-donors. For strong-field ligands, the crystal-field splitting Δo is larger, so the metal d electrons occupy lower-energy orbitals and avoid the higher antibonding levels. For weak-field ligands, the splitting is smaller and metal d electrons are pushed into higher-energy orbitals, including levels labeled t2g* in the diagram. The scheme also indicates σ interaction and π effects from the ligands, emphasizing that ligand electronics strongly control orbital energies, electron distribution, and overall complex stability.",
